sire to quit it  so this is what i do for many different  tasks and projects in my life i have  this list of skills really well broken  down so things that i want to do  everything that i see and i approach in  my life i kind of try to see what skills  are involved in it cross-reference them  with things that i value and then use  the task that i need to do and i don't  want to do as just a vehicle of  improving myself instead of focusing on  the thing and the things that people  tell me to care about like making more  money and passing exams and getting  numbers here and there this tends to be  very helpful for me i know it sounds  quite selfish and self-focused but it  really really helps in fueling  motivation for things that i would  otherwise be completely unable to find  so my second tool involves not quitting  a task through preserving motivation and  keeping an eye on the progress that i am  making and the strategies to avoid not  doing things that don't move the needle  that's a mouthful let me try and break  it down so progress has been identified  to be a major source of motivation and  this is both a positive and a very  dangerous thing because especially in  the cases where we are actually doing  work but we are not seeing any results  or not seeing the results that progress  that we want we can be in a place where  it's very very easy to have no  motivation and to absolutely want to  quit something and therefore i think  very often especially me in my life i  have set myself up for failure or at  least made things so much harder for me  to do and not giving myself a proper  shot at something that i could have  potentially been good at and i  eventually ended up quitting because i  have not been strategic about it in the  beginning this tool is called always  meet your heroes i'm going to use an  example of youtubers here when i first  started my youtube channel and  especially before starting it i thought  that what it took to become a very  successful youtuber in terms of skills  for educational youtube would be to be  quite confident to be quite charismatic  to be very funny and interesting and a  very super passionate and extroverted  person when having met a lot of  youtubers with a few million subscribers  at this point and seeing how they are in  person is very very different to how i  imagined them to be and actually the  skills that they were scoring very  highly on were neuroticism obsession  introversion ability to sit down and  tweak things on their computers for days  and days and so i realized that they  were very very different to what i  thought that they would be in person and  this kind of led me to the realization  in many different times in my life  actually that the skills that i think  that are important to succeed in a  certain task or domain are very often  very different to the ones that are  actually necessary and therefore when i  meet people that are my heroes or people  who are very successful in these fields  they end up being very confusing and a  very disappointing in a way experience  when i realized that they're so  different because i thought that it  would mean that you need to be so funny  to be successful at this when actually  what you need is to be kind of  neurotically obsessed at things which is  a completely different set of skills and  so i think meeting your heroes is a  perspective for thinking wait what is  actually necessary to succeed at this  task and what are the skills that  genuinely move the needle because we  don't want to be quote very long term in  a task that we don't enjoy doing things  that don't move the needle because then  we're wasting energy not making progress  and we are really putting our motivation  at risk so especially with tasks that i  already want to quit and tasks that i  know i'm not likely to enjoy a lot i  very quickly in the beginning will make  an assessment of what is going to move  the needle and that doesn't mean that i  only do the painful work that i don't  enjoy but it does mean that i never  completely ignore the skills and the  things that are absolutely essential for  this task because basically overnight  and with the same amount of work i can  get so much better results at something  if i focus on the things that actually  matter for it rather than what i feel  like doing or approaching things not  strategically and this has been such a  huge change for me because i did this  for the longest time and absolutely  everything i focused on the wrong things  so in any sort of serious task  especially when we are wanting to quit  it does well to us to look at the people  who are performing best and if possible  communicate with them or just otherwise  really kind of think about the task  critically and realize what are the  things that are actually the most  important here and start focusing on  those for a while we are likely then to  see results which are much much better  and with progress comes increased  motivation which then keeps our desire  to quit at bay and we are doing much  better at this task without wanting to  leave it my third tool is a very  elizabeth specific one so if you don't  resonate to my way of thinking you might  want to skip to the fourth one because  this might not be relevant but this is  basically a visual perspective that i  keep in mind when i am approaching any  sort of task that i am taking and  actually when i'm approaching anything  in my life in order to maintain  consistent motivation for long periods  of time i can be quite a passionate and  obsessive person which has its good  sides but also has a lot of downsides so  on the positive end it means that i  learn things super fast i get very very  involved and i get super passionate and  i can do work with very very good  results for some periods of time when  i'm invested in something but on the  negative side it means that i am very  often put in the weeds and i completely  lose perspective and i think i'm on a  path that if i'm starting to not enjoy i  feel completely trapped and desperate  and as though i have no solutions and i  can very quickly spiral into a lot of  anxiety around that task in general what  i do here is something that i call  strategic detachment and this is  basically where i'm keeping my  attachment and my passion in terms of  the motivation and how intensely i  perform at a task but i'm becoming quite  detached intentionally from my  associations with the tasks and my  identity and trying to keep an overall  perspective so visually the way that i  see this is that very often for many  different tasks or projects and they can  be big ones or small ones like i'm going  to medical school i feel like i'm on  this path of becoming a doctor and if i  take upon a task or responsibility i  feel like i'm on the path of completing  it and even if i read a book sometimes  as i've started i might see myself as  being on the path of finishing this book  and if i don't enjoy this thing i feel  trapped and very very strongly more and  more as i want to completely escape when  quitting is not probably the best  solution here the solution is changing  my perspective on everything so the way  that i see things now is that i am never  on the path of any sort of task that i  need to do and this might sound  grandiose so i apologize but i am on my  path and my skills path so everything  that i'm moving towards is comprised  just of me my future and the skills that  i want to keep on it and everything that  i do is just a vehicle to improve these  skills and myself and it's not the  actual path that i'm on so i'm not  becoming a doctor in the sense that i'm  on the path of doing that i'm becoming  elizabeth and being a doctor is just a  way for me to spend my time learning  about the human body and helping people  and kind of getting to get closer to  people and do things that i enjoy it's  just a vehicle for me to get to this  goal and when i start a book or when i  start a youtube channel i recognize that  all of these things are temporary i'm  probably going to leave youtube soon and  all of these different stuff are  happening these are just ways for me to  improve skills that i care about  communication skills connection skills  empathy skills how i want to spend my  time and my life is my perspective on  these things and i realize that this  feels like a bit of a selfish approach  and practically it does not change  things if anything it's helped me stop  quitting so many things by just  realizing i'm not trapped here this is  just something that i'm doing in order  to get somewhere else and so i know this  is a bit of a wishy-washy one but if we  can i would genuinely strongly recommend  doing the exercise of finding your  skills and the things that you value and  focusing on them and yourself and the  task and kind of ignoring the tasks that  you do and not feeling that you're  sucked into them that can help you not  want to quit them in the long term  because you realize that you're valuing  them only for the things that they are  and they are more temporary and fleeting  and we are never genuinely stuck  somewhere at least in our minds we can  always not be stuck somewhere which i  find to be insanely liberating lastly i  think that a strong trigger to want to  quit something for me is performing  badly at it consistently or not  progressing in this thing i realized  that actually there's a perspective that  i can change in order to switch this up  very very fast and for me that's called  instinct building so basically i think  that instincts are something that are  massively underrated instinct is that  ability that we can't put into words for  why we know something but we actually  know that that is the case so for  example when i'm in hospital i see that  nurses instinctively know when a patient  is acutely unwell even though they can't  tell us or me specifically why that is  they just know and that's an instinct of  having seen this thing very many times  sometimes i know that it's a good or bad  time for me to do things or what to do  even without being able to explain why  that's the case i just simply know it  because i have enough experience with  myself so i think that when i am not  progressing and i'm feeling like  quitting telling myself that actually  what's happening right now is that if i  just become a bit more intentional and i  realize what's happening this is not  futile what i'm doing is increasing my  instinct in the skills that i value in  that task and instinct is so so so  invaluable the reason for that is you  can only get it through painful  experience no one can pay to get your  instinct from you they can pay to get  you a response but they can't get it we  can only accrue instinct for ourselves  no one can take it away from us and no  one can give it to us and so it is an  absolutely invaluable skill i truly  believe that any long-term intentional  time i spent on a task is making me an  expert at that task and so  intentionality is something that i focus  on a lot i try to create short feedback  loops for everything that i do whenever  i'm doing a task even if it's completely  boring or i'm performing badly at it i  try to think how every small thing i do  leads to a response i try to be  intentional about this when i do things  this way this ends up when i do things  that way this ends up happening and i  see these feedback loops again and again  mistakes are a hugely positive thing to  frame in this way and seeing as this  growing my instinct and then in the  future investing in me becoming either  better at this task or at the skills  that this task involves i'm basically  being an expert in an extra field in my  life and the other better thing here is  that when we build instinct and  intuition at a task what we're doing is  fueling a source of endless motivation  and joy because we really really like to  do things that we can do instinctively  because they give us this sort of like  dopamine pleasure of going oh i'm in the  flow i understand what's happening i'm  feeling really really good and therefore  i'm investing in the future and not  wanting to quit other tasks and making  all other tasks that i do kind of easier  through increasing my intuition in the  skills that i care about and so the  skills i mentioned earlier i keep in  mind as being the things that i want to  become better at instinctively and  therefore because i then see all future  tasks that i do as a vehicle of  improving these skills i'm in this  positive loop of the older i get the  more i work the less i want to quit  things the more i can make myself  interested in things easier and the  better everything becomes and it just  becomes this positive cycle of  improvement which is so so so awesome  and it feels absolutely amazing i think  quite ironically this instinctual thing  is what i'm trying to do with this video  where i'm trying to break down every way  that i see things in my life and trying  to transfer that into a structure and a  format and a template that can hopefully  help other people too every framework  that i've said in this video i've broken  down in a lot more detail with guiding  questions in a guide below that also has  this template of being able to start  projects and then fill them in with  these tools and then watch your progress  with them there's no gatekeeping of  information or secrets in that template  it's basically everything that i've said  in much more detail and structured with  a kind of project list and a way to  apply this again and again so if you  want to check that out it will be linked  in my description otherwise thank you so  much for spending this time with me i  hope you have a wonderful rest of the  day become yourself and others and don't  believe everything you think thanks bye
 

